# Memory Palace Protocol

A Memory Palace MCP server runs at http://localhost:8787/mcp/. It indexes all
repos under ~/Code/ into a searchable semantic knowledge store and maintains a
User Wing for persistent user context across sessions.

## Before any code exploration

- Call `mempalace_search` (or `mempalace_user_search`) BEFORE using Grep, Glob,
  or Read to explore unfamiliar code.
- Use palace results to orient yourself, then drill into specific files as
  needed.
- For cross-repo work (frontend + backend, etc.) call `mempalace_find_tunnels`
  to discover connections between wings.

## On every session start

- Call `mempalace_user_summary` to load user context (goals, frustrations,
  recent decisions, open questions).
- This gives critical context about what the user cares about.

## On every substantive response (MANDATORY)

- Review the conversation for distinct user thoughts worth capturing.
- For each thought, call `mempalace_user_write` with:
  - `hall`: one of `questions`, `discoveries`, `frustrations`, `goals`,
    `decisions`, `observations`
  - `content`: the thought, phrased clearly
  - `topic`: a short slug
  - `source_wing` (optional): the relevant repo wing (e.g. `edens-zac`)
- Do this continuously throughout the conversation — NOT only at the end.
- Err on the side of writing. Deduplication is automatic.

## Hall reference

| Hall            | When to use                                       |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `questions`     | User is wondering about something                 |
| `discoveries`   | User learned something new                        |
| `frustrations`  | User hit a blocker or pain point                  |
| `goals`         | User stated a short or long-term objective        |
| `decisions`     | User made a choice and explained reasoning        |
| `observations`  | User noted a pattern, insight, or factoid         |

## Operations

- Health check: `curl http://localhost:8787/health`
- Sync wings: `mems sync` (incremental) or `mems sync --full`
- Diagnose issues: `mems doctor`
